Network Types: LAN, WAN, and WLAN


Networks are classified into different types based on the area they cover and the method of connection. The most basic network types are LAN, WAN, and WLAN.

What Is a LAN?

LAN stands for Local Area Network.

It is a type of network in which devices within a home, school, office, or small building are connected to one another.

Examples:

Computers at home connected to the same modem
Computers in an office operating on the same network
Devices in a school computer lab connected to the same network

Simple diagram:

Computer → Router/Switch → Printer
Phone    → Router/Switch → Internet
What Is a WLAN?

WLAN stands for Wireless Local Area Network.

It can be thought of as the wireless version of a LAN. It is typically set up using Wi-Fi.

Example:

Connecting my phone or laptop to the Wi-Fi network at home is an example of a WLAN.

Simple diagram:

Phone / Laptop → Wi-Fi → Router
What is WAN?

WAN stands for Wide Area Network.

It is a type of network that connects networks located in different cities, countries, or remote regions.

The Internet is one of the largest examples of a WAN.

Simple illustration:

Home Network → Router → Internet Service Provider → Internet
The Difference Between LAN, WLAN, and WAN
LAN = Local Area Network
WLAN = Wireless Local Area Network
WAN = Wide Area Network

Example:

When my phone connects to Wi-Fi at home, it uses a WLAN. Since my phone is on the same local network as other devices in the house, it is part of the LAN. When connecting to the Internet, it goes through the router to the WAN side.

Importance in Cybersecurity

Understanding network types is important in cybersecurity because each type of network poses different security risks.

In LAN security:

Protecting devices on the local network
Preventing unauthorized devices from connecting to the network
Switch, router, and firewall configurations are important.

In WLAN security:

Wi-Fi password
Use of WPA2/WPA3
Rogue access point attacks
Monitoring Wi-Fi traffic are important topics.

In WAN security:

Threats originating from the Internet
Firewall
VPN
DDoS attacks
Remote connection security are important topics.
Summary in My Own Words

A LAN is a local network consisting of devices within a small area. A WLAN is the wireless (Wi-Fi) version of this local network. A WAN, on the other hand, is a wide-area network that connects remote networks to one another. When I use Wi-Fi at home, I’m on the WLAN; my home network is the LAN, and when I connect to the internet, I connect to the WAN.
